Understood. I need jq (textproc/jq) in lab-mail.

Why: smtprelay only takes its remote as a URL, so the Fastmail login
and app password must be percent-encoded into the userinfo part. The
login has an "@", and an app password may hold ":", "/" or "@", any
of which breaks the URL. FreeBSD base has no URL encoder. jq's @uri
filter does exactly this, and jq is a small general tool we are
likely to want elsewhere.

What I checked (fact): pkg search finds jq-1.8.2 in the repo; it is
not installed on lab-claude. Besides jq, the repo has only Perl
modules for form encoding (p5-URL-Encode and friends), which pull in
Perl for one function. jq's manual says @uri maps every reserved URI
character to a %XX sequence; I have not run it here, so the exact
output is untested.

With jq, the 42-line userinfo script goes away. remotes.env would
build the remote directly, splitting the line with cut:

    login=$(cut -d' ' -f1 /data/secrets/mail-creds | jq -Rr @uri)
    pass=$(cut -d' ' -f2- /data/secrets/mail-creds | jq -Rr @uri)
    SMTPRELAY_REMOTES="smtps://${login}:${pass}@smtp.fastmail.com:465"

and instance.conf adds textproc/jq to the packages. If you approve jq,
that is what v9 will do.
